# Build output. Every generated asset a site has now lands under
# sites/<name>/build/: the Vite bundle, the blog's post PDFs and social cards,
# and analytics' topojson. One ignored directory per site instead of five
# scattered through the source, and one place to look when the question is
# "what here did I write, and what did a tool write".
#
# It sits inside the site rather than at the repo root because //go:embed
# cannot reference a path above its own package directory, and a release build
# embeds all of it.
build/
bin/
node_modules/

# Generated by `bun run images` from the committed sources in frontend/images/.
# Build output, not source: 22 variants that would otherwise churn the repo
# every time a quality setting moved.
/sites/*/frontend/public/images/

# `go build .` inside a site directory writes an executable named after that
# directory, which is how a 14MB binary got committed on day one. `make build`
# uses -o bin/ and avoids this, but the bare command is the one people type.
/sites/*/isaacbythewood.com
/sites/*/*.com
/sites/*/*.me
!/sites/*.com/
!/sites/*.me/

# The same trap sprung from the repo root: `go build ./sites/<name>/` writes
# the executable into the working directory, not next to its source, so the
# rules above do not catch it. Found when the analytics port left a 27MB
# binary here.
/*.com
/*.me

# Same rule for the resume: resume/content.yml and resume/resume.typ are the
# source, this is the output. It used to be committed, back when generating it
# meant running a headless Chromium by hand.
/sites/*/frontend/public/pdfs/
/sites/*/frontend/public/og/

# Playwright scratch
.playwright-cli/

# Tunnel credentials never touch the repo: they live in the named volume that
# setup-tunnel.sh creates.
credentials.json
cert.pem
*.pem
.env

# Runtime state, not source: the SQLite database and the 131MB GeoIP mmdb the
# server downloads on boot. In production these live in a named Docker volume.
/sites/*/data/

# Opt-in local port publishing for eyeballing a site before its DNS exists.
# Machine-specific, never part of a deploy.
docker-compose.local.yml
